{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## OPERATOR DECISION (2026-08-24) — remote-anchored GC + stale-clone override; promote to epic\n\nStability model chosen: **option 2 (remote-anchored horizon)** combined with a **new mechanism\nthat forces any clone more than N days stale to override its local state with the remote state**.\nThe residual risk — **losing information from a clone that has drifted for more than the horizon\n(target N = 30 days) — is explicitly ACCEPTED.**\n\n### What this means concretely\n- **GC**: a `.retired` tombstone (and its folded companion) becomes eligible for removal only once\n  its folding compaction commit has been reachable from the **sync remote's `tickets` ref for >= N\n  days**. Any clone that pushes/pulls converges through the remote, so within the horizon every\n  live clone has seen the compaction.\n- **Stale-clone override (the piece that makes GC provably safe rather than probabilistic)**: a\n  clone whose last convergence with the remote is older than N days does **not** get to reintroduce\n  pre-compaction events by rebase; on reconnect it is **forced to override local state with the\n  remote**. This closes the I1 hole (a long-offline clone resurrecting a folded source) that made\n  a pure time/remote horizon merely probabilistic — the override converts \"no clone *should* be\n  partitioned longer than N days\" into an *enforced* invariant, at the accepted cost of discarding\n  a >N-day-drifted clone's divergent local work.\n\n### Why this is an ADR + epic, not a task\nThis changes concurrency doctrine (`docs/concurrency.md` §I1 and the invariants I1–I9) and the\nmerge/rebase contract, and adds a new stale-clone-override mechanism. Per epic b306\n(adept-hedge-stain) permanent retention was the *status quo tradeoff*; reversing it is a doctrine\nchange. **Promoting this task to an epic.** Required child work:\n- **S0 (ADR)** — \"Remote-anchored retired-event GC + stale-clone override (N-day horizon)\":\n  amend/supersede the §I1 \"permanent retention\" doctrine; define the horizon config key (default\n  30d, portable), the remote-reachability check, the stale-clone detection + forced-override\n  protocol, and prove the I1–I9 invariants hold under it (with the accepted >N-day loss stated).\n- **S1** — remote-anchored eligibility check for `.retired` GC (the horizon computation).\n- **S2** — the branch-wide GC sweep itself (portable; operation/test-linked, not CI-specific),\n  honoring the eligibility check.\n- **S3** — stale-clone detection + forced remote-override on reconnect.\n- Then **degradable-appalling-cavy**'s retired-sidecar reclaim (D1) depends on S1/S2.\n\nDecomposing into these child slices next. No code touched here."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787592900281798001,"uuid":"5227572f-2566-47e0-bdd7-9f3f92b9f872"}